multichoice[20]{tags,text,exhibit,correct,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.8|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:personnel-security","An employee is dismissed for cause. Which action should happen first?",,"Disable the employee's accounts and physical access at the time of notification, then escort the employee from the premises","Wait until the end of the notice period to disable access","Ask the employee to delete personal data from the company laptop","Move the employee's files to a shared drive before access is revoked","A dismissal for cause is a risk of sabotage or data theft, so access ends when the person is told. Waiting or leaving access in place gives the employee time to cause harm.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.11|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:scrm","A supplier's router firmware is found to contain a hidden backdoor. Which supply chain risk management control would most likely have detected the tampering?",,"Verifying signed firmware and buying only from authorized sources, with provenance records or an SBOM","A yearly penetration test of the supplier's website","A longer warranty on the hardware","Downloading the firmware over TLS","Signed firmware and trusted sourcing show that the code came from the vendor unchanged, and provenance records help to trace components. A website test, a warranty or a TLS download do not prove that the firmware is untampered.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:hipaa|topic:privacy","Which organizations are directly subject to the HIPAA Security Rule?",,"Covered entities such as health plans and providers, and their business associates","Any company that sells consumer goods online","Only government agencies","Only the individuals whose records are stored","HIPAA applies to covered entities that handle protected health information and to the business associates that process it for them. Ordinary retailers, government agencies as a class and patients are not the regulated parties.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-management","A business-critical server runs software that the vendor no longer supports. Which response best balances risk?",,"Plan its replacement and, until then, isolate the server and apply compensating controls","Keep it as it is, because it still works","Switch it off at once without a replacement plan","Buy a longer hardware warranty","Unsupported software gets no security fixes, so the risk grows. A planned replacement with isolation and compensating controls reduces risk while keeping the business running. Doing nothing or an abrupt shutdown ignores either risk or the business need.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-inventory","Which approach gives the most reliable and current asset inventory?",,"Automated discovery reconciled with records that name an owner for each asset","A spreadsheet that purchasing updates once a year","Voluntary reports from each department","Counting assets only when they are disposed of","Automated discovery finds what is really on the network, and reconciliation with owned records exposes gaps. Manual or occasional methods go out of date quickly.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:scoping|topic:tailoring","Which terms describe removing the controls of a baseline that do not apply to a system, and then adjusting the remaining ones to the organization's risk?",,"Scoping, then tailoring","Tokenization, then masking","Sanitization, then destruction","Classification, then labeling","Scoping decides which controls of a baseline apply to a system, and tailoring modifies the selected controls to fit the organization's risk and environment. The other pairs describe different activities.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:zero-trust","Which statement best describes zero trust?",,"No user or device is trusted by default, and each request is verified using identity, device state and context, with access limited to what is needed","Internal network traffic is trusted and only external traffic is inspected","A single strong perimeter firewall replaces the other controls","Users are trusted for the whole day after the first successful login","Zero trust removes implicit trust based on network location and verifies every access request. Trusting the internal network or a single login is the model that zero trust replaces.",2,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:tpm","Which component provides tamper-resistant storage of keys and measurements of the boot process on a computer's motherboard?",,"Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","Rack-mounted Hardware Security Module (HSM)","Network interface card","BIOS password","A TPM is a chip on the board that stores keys and platform measurements and can attest to the boot state. An HSM is a separate appliance for cryptographic operations, and a BIOS password is only an access control.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cloud|topic:shared-responsibility","In an IaaS deployment, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system?",,"The customer","The cloud provider","The hardware vendor","The regulator","In IaaS the provider manages the facility, hardware and hypervisor, and the customer manages everything above, including the guest operating system and its patches.",2,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ipv6","Which IPv6 address type takes the place of IPv4 broadcast?",,"Multicast","Unicast","Anycast","Link-local","IPv6 has no broadcast, and multicast addresses deliver to a group of interested hosts. Unicast is one-to-one, anycast goes to the nearest of several hosts, and link-local is an address scope.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:transmission-media","Which transmission medium is immune to electromagnetic interference and hardest to tap without detection?",,"Fiber optic cable","Unshielded twisted pair","Coaxial cable","Wi-Fi","Fiber carries light, so it does not pick up electromagnetic interference and does not radiate signals, and tapping it disturbs the light. Copper cables radiate and can be tapped, and Wi-Fi is broadcast.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:access-review","An audit finds that badges of terminated employees still open the data center door. Which process failed?",,"Deprovisioning and periodic access review","Identity proofing","Session management","Federation","Access must be removed when people leave and checked regularly, and both safeguards failed. Proofing, session handling and federation concern other stages.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:rbac","A company wants permissions to follow job functions and to make audits simpler as people move between teams. Which model fits best?",,"Role-based access control (RBAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Rule-based access control","RBAC assigns permissions to roles, so moving a person means changing a role and an auditor reviews roles instead of individuals. MAC uses labels, DAC leaves access to owners, and rule-based control applies global rules.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:credential-management|topic:secrets","Why should a service account's password be kept in a credential vault instead of in a script?",,"The vault limits who can read it, can rotate it, and records each use","A script cannot read passwords","A vault makes the password unnecessary","A vault encrypts the network between hosts","Vaults control and log access to secrets and support rotation. A password in a script is visible to everyone who can read it and is hard to change.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:backup","Which result is the strongest evidence that backups can be used for recovery?",,"A successful restore into an isolated environment, with the data checked","The backup job reports completion","The backup software shows green status","The backup volume has free space","Only a restore proves that the data can be read and is complete. Completion messages, status lights and free space say nothing about recoverability.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:pentest","Which test is most likely to find a business-logic flaw that automated scanners miss?",,"A manual penetration test by an experienced tester","An automated port scan","A vulnerability scan with default settings","A checksum comparison of the binaries","Business-logic flaws depend on how an application is meant to work, which a human can reason about, while scanners look for known patterns. Port scans, default scans and checksums do not test logic.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:job-rotation","A bank requires employees in sensitive roles to take a two-week vacation during which others do their work. What is the main security purpose?",,"To detect fraud or irregularities that the employee would otherwise hide","To lower the cost of health insurance","To enforce least privilege","To improve availability of the staff","Mandatory vacations and job rotation let someone else see the work, which exposes fraud that depends on a single person's continuous control. They do not enforce least privilege.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.8|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:patching","A vendor announces a fix for a vulnerability that is being actively exploited. What is the best way to apply it?",,"Use the expedited change process: test quickly, have a rollback plan, and deploy first to the most exposed systems","Wait for the next quarterly maintenance window","Skip testing because the vendor released the fix","Apply it everywhere at once without a rollback plan","Active exploitation raises urgency but not the need for control, so an expedited process with quick testing, rollback and a priority order balances speed and safety. Waiting is too slow, and skipping tests or rollback risks outages.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:memory-safe","Which language property most directly prevents buffer overflow vulnerabilities?",,"Memory safety, with automatic bounds checking","Object-oriented design","Case-sensitive variable names","A large standard library","Memory-safe languages check array and buffer bounds, so code cannot write past the end of a buffer. Object orientation, naming rules and libraries do not provide that protection.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:metrics","Which measure best shows that an organization's software security is improving?",,"A falling trend in vulnerabilities found per release and in the time taken to fix them","The number of security tools purchased","The number of lines of code written","The number of developers trained last year","Outcomes such as fewer vulnerabilities and faster remediation show better security. Tools bought, code written and training counts measure activity, not results.",4,3

multiselect[20]{tags,text,exhibit,c1,c2,c3,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:threat-modeling","Which two are threat modeling methodologies? (Choose two.)",,"STRIDE","PASTA",,"ITIL","Clark-Wilson","TOGAF","STRIDE categorizes threats, and PASTA is a risk-centric process for threat modeling. ITIL is a service management framework, Clark-Wilson is an integrity model, and TOGAF is an enterprise architecture framework.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.12|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:awareness","Which two measures best show the effectiveness of a security awareness program? (Choose two.)",,"The click rate in phishing simulations over time","The share of suspicious messages that staff report, and how quickly",,"The number of slides in the training deck","The number of people who attended","The length of the training video","Effectiveness is about changed behavior, such as fewer clicks on simulated phishing and faster reporting. Attendance and content size measure effort, not results.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:bia","Which two statements about a business impact analysis (BIA) are correct? (Choose two.)",,"It identifies critical business processes and their dependencies","It produces the maximum tolerable downtime for each critical process",,"It selects the vendor of the recovery site","It is performed after the disaster recovery test","It covers only IT systems","A BIA finds which processes matter most, what they depend on, and how long they can be down. Vendor choice follows from its results, it comes before testing, and it covers business processes beyond IT.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:retention","Which two statements about data retention are correct? (Choose two.)",,"Retention periods are set by legal, regulatory and business needs","Data that is no longer required should be destroyed securely when the period ends",,"Data should be kept forever to be safe","Backups are exempt from retention rules","Retention applies only to paper records","Keeping data too long raises cost and exposure, so periods follow legal and business needs and data is destroyed at the end. Backups and electronic records are included.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:masking|topic:tokenization","Which two techniques reduce the exposure of sensitive data in a test environment? (Choose two.)",,"Masking or obfuscating the sensitive fields","Replacing the values with tokens",,"Copying the production data unchanged","Disabling logging in the test system","Giving every tester administrator rights","Masking and tokenization give testers realistic data without the real values. Copying production data exposes it, and the other options reduce accountability.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:containers","Which two are risks that are specific to containerized workloads? (Choose two.)",,"Containers share the host kernel, so a kernel flaw affects them all","Images from untrusted registries can contain vulnerabilities or malware",,"Every container needs its own full guest operating system","Containers cannot be scanned for vulnerabilities","Containers cannot use network segmentation","Shared kernels and untrusted images are the characteristic risks. Containers do not need a full guest operating system, and they can be scanned and segmented.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.8|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cpted","Which three are principles of crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED)? (Choose three.)",,"Natural surveillance","Natural access control","Territorial reinforcement","Hiding entrances from view","Placing the parking area behind the fence line","Removing all lighting at night","CPTED shapes the environment so that legitimate users can see and control the space, which deters offenders. Hiding entrances, unlit areas and poorly placed parking weaken surveillance.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:digital-signatures","Which two statements about digital signatures are correct? (Choose two.)",,"The sender signs a hash of the message with the sender's private key","The receiver verifies the signature with the sender's public key",,"The sender signs with the receiver's public key","A digital signature provides confidentiality of the message","The receiver verifies with the receiver's own private key","A signature is made with the signer's private key and verified with the matching public key, which gives integrity, authentication and non-repudiation. It does not encrypt the message.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sdn","Which two statements about software-defined networking (SDN) are correct? (Choose two.)",,"The control plane is separated from the data plane","A controller programs the devices through APIs",,"Each device makes its own forwarding decisions independently","SDN requires every device to run a different operating system","SDN removes the need for any network security","SDN centralizes control in a controller that programs the forwarding devices through interfaces. Independent decisions describe traditional networks, and SDN still needs security.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:voip","Which two controls help to protect VoIP traffic? (Choose two.)",,"Encrypting media with SRTP","Placing voice traffic on its own VLAN",,"Sending calls over an open guest network","Disabling authentication on phones","Using default passwords on handsets","SRTP protects calls from eavesdropping and a separate VLAN limits exposure. Open networks, no authentication and default passwords weaken the system.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:wireless","Which two are improvements that WPA3 brings over WPA2? (Choose two.)",,"The Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) handshake resists offline password guessing","Each session has its own keys, so recorded traffic cannot be decrypted later with the password",,"It uses the WEP cipher for compatibility","It transmits the password in clear text during the handshake","It removes the need for any password on all networks","SAE replaces the pre-shared key handshake and protects against offline guessing, and per-session keys provide forward secrecy. WPA3 does not use WEP or send the password.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sso","Which two statements about single sign-on (SSO) are correct? (Choose two.)",,"It reduces the number of passwords that users must manage","The identity provider becomes a high-value target and a single point of failure",,"It removes the need for authentication","It eliminates the risk of stolen credentials","It works only inside one application","SSO improves usability and centralizes authentication, which also concentrates risk. Users still authenticate once, and stolen credentials remain a risk.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:service-accounts","Which two practices manage service accounts well? (Choose two.)",,"Disable interactive logon for the account","Store its credential in a vault and rotate it",,"Share the account with the administration team","Give it domain administrator rights for convenience","Never review who owns it","Service accounts should not be used by people, should have a protected and rotated credential, and should have the least rights and a named owner. Sharing and excessive rights are common causes of compromise.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:soc","Which two statements about SOC reports are correct? (Choose two.)",,"A SOC 2 report evaluates controls against the Trust Services Criteria","A SOC 3 report is a general-use summary that can be shared publicly",,"A SOC 1 report covers the availability of any cloud service","A SOC 2 report is intended for public marketing only","A Type I report tests operation over a year","SOC 2 is based on the Trust Services Criteria, and SOC 3 is a public summary. SOC 1 addresses financial reporting, and a Type I report looks at design at a point in time.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:fuzzing","Which two statements about fuzzing are correct? (Choose two.)",,"It sends malformed or random input to a program","It can reveal crashes and vulnerabilities that normal tests miss",,"It reads source code without running it","It works only against compiled languages","It replaces the need for code review","Fuzzing is a dynamic technique that feeds unexpected input and watches for failures. It is not static analysis, it applies to many kinds of programs, and it complements other methods.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:media-protection","Which two controls protect portable storage media? (Choose two.)",,"Encrypting the media","Keeping an inventory with labels and a custodian",,"Storing it unlabeled in a shared drawer","Allowing any device to mount any media","Disabling endpoint controls for removable drives","Encryption protects the data if the media is lost, and inventory and labels create accountability. The other options remove control.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.11|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:disaster-recovery","Which two items belong in a disaster recovery plan? (Choose two.)",,"Contact lists and the communication methods to use","The priority order in which systems are restored",,"The annual marketing budget","A list of past employee reviews","The color scheme of the intranet","A recovery plan states who to contact and what to restore first. The other items have no role in recovery.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.15|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:travel","Which two actions reduce the risk of traveling to a high-risk country with company data? (Choose two.)",,"Carry a loaner laptop with only the data needed","Use a VPN and avoid public charging ports and unknown networks",,"Take the normal laptop with full access to every system","Connect to any free Wi-Fi to save roaming costs","Leave the laptop unlocked in the hotel room","A clean loaner limits what can be lost or seized, and a VPN and careful charging reduce exposure. The other actions raise the risk.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:api-security","Which two controls help to secure an API? (Choose two.)",,"Authenticate and authorize every request, for example with OAuth 2.0 tokens","Apply rate limiting and validate all input",,"Return detailed stack traces to clients","Disable TLS inside the internal network","Allow unauthenticated access to administrative endpoints","Per-request authentication and authorization plus rate limiting and input validation are core API controls. Detailed errors, missing TLS and open administrative endpoints help attackers.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:repositories|topic:secrets","Which two controls protect a source code repository? (Choose two.)",,"Branch protection that requires reviews before merging","Secret scanning that blocks committed credentials",,"Giving every developer administrator rights on the repository","Storing production passwords in the repository for convenience","Disabling the audit log","Required reviews and secret scanning reduce the chance of malicious or careless changes. Excessive rights, stored passwords and disabled logs weaken the repository.",3,2

match[20]{tags,text,exhibit,p1,r1,p2,r2,p3,r3,p4,r4,p5,r5,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:frameworks","Match each framework or standard to its description.",,"ISO/IEC 27001","International standard for an information security management system","NIST CSF","Voluntary framework organized around functions such as identify, protect and detect","COBIT","Framework for governance and management of enterprise IT","SABSA","Framework for enterprise security architecture driven by business needs","PCI DSS","Requirements for organizations that handle payment card data","These frameworks serve different purposes: certifiable management systems, a risk-based framework, IT governance, security architecture, and card data protection.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:privacy|topic:laws","Match each law to its subject.",,"GDPR","Protection of personal data of people in the EU","HIPAA","Protection of health information in the United States","CCPA","Privacy rights of California residents","PIPL","Protection of personal information in China","POPIA","Protection of personal information in South Africa","Each law is tied to a region or sector, which decides who must comply and what rights individuals have.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cia","Match each security property to an example of a control that supports it.",,"Confidentiality","Encrypting data on a laptop","Integrity","Hashing a file to detect changes","Availability","Redundant power and network paths","Authenticity","A certificate that proves a server's identity","Non-repudiation","A digital signature on a contract","Each property has typical supporting controls, from encryption and hashing to redundancy, certificates and signatures.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-protection","Match each data protection technique to its description.",,"Masking","Hides part of a value, such as showing only the last four digits","Tokenization","Replaces a value with a random surrogate kept in a secure mapping","Anonymization","Irreversibly removes identifying details","Pseudonymization","Replaces identifiers but allows re-identification with extra information","Encryption","Transforms data with a key so that only key holders can read it","The techniques differ in whether the change can be reversed and who can reverse it.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.3|objective:isc2:cissp:2.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-management","Match each asset management term to its meaning.",,"Asset inventory","A record of the organization's tangible and intangible assets","Asset owner","The person accountable for an asset and its protection","CMDB","Database of configuration items and their relationships","End of life (EOL)","The vendor stops selling the product","End of support (EOS)","The vendor stops providing updates and fixes","The terms cover recording assets, assigning responsibility, tracking relationships, and the end of a product's commercial and support life.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cloud","Match each cloud model to its description.",,"IaaS","The provider delivers virtual machines, storage and networks","PaaS","The provider delivers a platform for running applications","SaaS","The provider delivers a complete application","FaaS","Code runs as functions that are triggered by events","Private cloud","Infrastructure used by a single organization","The models differ in how much the customer manages, from the infrastructure to the application, and in who uses the infrastructure.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:power","Match each power problem to its description.",,"Spike","A brief, sharp rise in voltage","Surge","A prolonged rise in voltage","Sag","A brief drop in voltage","Brownout","A prolonged drop in voltage","Blackout","A complete loss of power","Power problems are described by direction and duration, and each calls for protection such as surge protectors, line conditioners, UPS and generators.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:vpn","Match each VPN technology to its description.",,"IPsec","Network-layer VPN protocol suite using IKE and ESP","TLS VPN","Uses TLS and often works through a web browser or a client","WireGuard","Modern VPN protocol with a small code base and fixed cryptography","L2TP","Tunneling protocol that is usually combined with IPsec for encryption","PPTP","Obsolete tunneling protocol with known weaknesses","Each technology works at a different layer or has a different history, and PPTP should not be used.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:network-attacks","Match each network attack to its description.",,"ARP spoofing","Sends false ARP replies to redirect traffic","MAC flooding","Overflows a switch's address table so that it floods frames","VLAN hopping","Gets access to a VLAN other than the one assigned","DNS poisoning","Inserts false records into a resolver's cache","SYN flood","Exhausts a server's connection table with half-open connections","Each attack abuses a protocol or table of a different layer, and each has its own countermeasure.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:network-devices","Match each network component to its function.",,"Hub","Repeats signals to all ports","Switch","Forwards frames by MAC address","Router","Forwards packets between networks by IP address","Stateful firewall","Filters traffic using the state of connections","Web application firewall","Inspects HTTP traffic for application-layer attacks","The components work at increasing layers, from repeating signals to application inspection.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:authentication-attacks","Match each attack on authentication to its description.",,"Brute force","Tries many passwords against one account","Password spraying","Tries a few common passwords against many accounts","Credential stuffing","Reuses leaked username and password pairs on other sites","Pass the hash","Authenticates with a stolen password hash","Phishing","Tricks a user into giving credentials","The attacks differ in how credentials are obtained or guessed, and so in the best defenses, such as lockout, MFA and monitoring.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:biometrics","Match each biometric measure to its meaning.",,"False acceptance rate (FAR)","How often an unauthorized person is accepted","False rejection rate (FRR)","How often an authorized person is rejected","Crossover error rate (CER)","The point where FAR and FRR are equal","Enrollment","Capturing a person's biometric template","Throughput","How many people can be processed in a given time","The measures describe the accuracy and the usability of a biometric system. A lower crossover error rate means a more accurate system.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:directory","Match each identity term to its meaning.",,"LDAP","Protocol for querying and modifying a directory","SCIM","Standard for provisioning user accounts between systems","JIT provisioning","Creating an account at the first login","Identity provider","Authenticates users for other applications","Group","Collection of accounts that are given permissions together","The terms describe how directories are queried, how accounts are created and synchronized, and how users are grouped.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:coverage","Match each coverage measure to its description.",,"Statement coverage","The share of code lines that were executed","Branch coverage","The share of decision outcomes that were exercised","Function coverage","The share of functions that were called","Requirements coverage","The share of requirements with at least one test","Attack surface coverage","The share of exposed interfaces that were tested","Coverage analysis shows how much of the code, the requirements or the exposed interfaces the tests reached, and where gaps remain.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:reporting","Match each part of a security test report to its purpose.",,"Executive summary","Gives managers the overall result and the main risks","Findings","Describe each weakness with evidence","Risk rating","Shows how serious each finding is","Recommendations","Say how to fix or reduce each finding","Appendices","Hold detailed evidence, such as logs and screenshots","A good report serves several readers, from managers who need a summary to engineers who need evidence and fixes.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:logging","Match each logging or monitoring term to its meaning.",,"Syslog","Standard protocol for sending log messages to a collector","SIEM","Collects and correlates events from many sources","NetFlow","Records summaries of network conversations","UEBA","Detects unusual behavior of users and entities","Threat feed","Supplies indicators of known malicious activity","The terms describe how logs are transported, correlated, summarized, analyzed for behavior and enriched with threat information.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:forensics","Match each forensic term to its meaning.",,"Chain of custody","Documents who handled the evidence and when","Write blocker","Prevents changes to a drive while it is copied","Forensic image","A bit-for-bit copy of the original media","Hash value","Proves that a copy is identical to the original","Order of volatility","The sequence for collecting data that disappears fastest first","Forensics relies on preserving and proving the integrity of evidence, and on collecting the most volatile data first.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:operations","Match each foundational operations concept to its meaning.",,"Need to know","Access only to information that a task requires","Least privilege","Only the rights needed to perform the job","Separation of duties","No single person can complete a critical process alone","Job rotation","Staff change roles to reduce fraud and spread knowledge","Privileged account management","Controls and records the use of administrative accounts","These concepts limit what people can do and see, and make misuse harder to hide.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:maturity","Match each level of the Capability Maturity Model to its description.",,"Initial","Work is ad hoc and unpredictable","Repeatable","Basic project management lets success be repeated","Defined","Processes are documented and standardized","Managed","Processes are measured and controlled","Optimizing","Processes are continuously improved","The levels describe how disciplined and measured an organization's processes are, from ad hoc to continuously improving.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:acquired-software","Match each type of acquired software to its main security concern.",,"COTS","The organization cannot see or change the source code","Open source","Components may carry known vulnerabilities and license obligations","Third-party library","A flaw is inherited by every application that uses it","Managed service","Provider controls and contract terms decide the protection","SaaS","Data is processed on the provider's platform","Each type of acquired software moves control to someone else, and so changes what must be assessed and contracted.",2,2

order[20]{tags,text,exhibit,s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.12|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:awareness","Place the steps of running a security awareness program in the correct order.",,"Assess the needs and the risks","Develop the content","Deliver the training","Measure the effectiveness","Improve the program from the results",,"A program starts with needs, develops and delivers content, measures behavior and improves from what it learns.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:risk","Place the steps of a qualitative risk assessment in the correct order.",,"Identify the assets","Identify threats and vulnerabilities","Estimate likelihood and impact","Rank the risks","Choose the risk treatment",,"Assets, threats and vulnerabilities are identified before likelihood and impact are estimated, and the ranking guides the choice of treatment.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-management","Place the steps of securely provisioning a new server in the correct order.",,"Record it in the inventory and assign an owner","Harden it to the approved baseline","Apply the classification and labels","Deploy it","Monitor it",,"An asset is recorded and owned, hardened and labeled before it goes into service, and monitored afterwards.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-management","Place the steps of retiring an asset in the correct order.",,"Decide that the asset reaches end of life","Back up or migrate the data that is still needed","Sanitize the storage","Remove it from the inventory and the monitoring","Dispose of it according to policy",,"The data that is still needed is preserved first, the storage is sanitized, and records are updated before disposal.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:retention","Place the steps of managing the retention of a record in the correct order.",,"Classify the record","Set the retention period from legal and business needs","Store it securely and archive it when it is no longer active","Check for a legal hold at the end of the period","Destroy it securely",,"The period follows from classification and requirements, and a legal hold is checked before destruction.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:digital-signatures","Place the steps of signing and verifying a message in the correct order.",,"The sender computes a hash of the message","The sender encrypts the hash with the sender's private key","The sender sends the message and the signature","The receiver decrypts the signature with the sender's public key","The receiver computes a hash of the message","The receiver compares the two hashes","The sender hashes and signs, the receiver recovers the signed hash, computes its own and compares them.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:lifecycle","Place the steps of retiring an information system securely in the correct order.",,"Plan the retirement and notify the stakeholders","Archive the data that must be kept","Remove access and shut down the services","Sanitize or destroy the media","Update the records and the inventory",,"Retirement is planned, retained data is archived, access is removed, media is sanitized and the records are updated.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:tcp","Place the steps of the TCP three-way handshake in the correct order.",,"The client sends a SYN","The server answers with a SYN-ACK","The client sends an ACK","Data transfer begins",,,"The client opens with SYN, the server acknowledges with SYN-ACK, and the client confirms with ACK before data flows.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:dns","Place the steps of a recursive DNS lookup in the correct order.",,"The client asks its recursive resolver","The resolver asks a root server","The root server refers the resolver to the top-level domain server","The resolver asks the authoritative server","The resolver returns the answer to the client","The client connects to the address","The resolver walks down the hierarchy from the root to the authoritative server and returns the result to the client.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:firewall","Place the steps of a stateful firewall handling a new outbound connection in the correct order.",,"The first packet arrives","The firewall checks it against the rule base","It creates an entry in the state table","The return traffic is matched to the entry and allowed","The entry is removed when the connection closes",,"The first packet is checked against rules, state is recorded, return traffic is allowed by state, and the entry is deleted at the end.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:saml","Place the steps of a SAML web single sign-on in the correct order.",,"The user requests the service provider's resource","The service provider redirects the user to the identity provider","The user authenticates at the identity provider","The identity provider issues an assertion","The service provider validates the assertion","The user gets access","The service provider sends the user to the identity provider, which authenticates and returns an assertion that the service provider validates.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:kerberos","Place the steps of Kerberos authentication in the correct order.",,"The client authenticates to the authentication service and receives a ticket-granting ticket","The client presents the ticket-granting ticket to the ticket-granting service","The ticket-granting service issues a service ticket","The client presents the service ticket to the service","The service grants access",,"A ticket-granting ticket is issued once, and it is exchanged for service tickets that are presented to each service.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit","Place the steps of designing an assessment strategy in the correct order.",,"Define the objectives","Choose the type of assessment, internal, external or third-party","Set the scope and the criteria","Plan the resources and the schedule","Validate the strategy with the stakeholders",,"The objectives decide the type, scope and criteria, which are then planned and agreed with the stakeholders.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:backup","Place the steps of verifying a backup in the correct order.",,"Select a sample of backups","Restore it to an isolated environment","Check the integrity and the completeness of the data","Record the results","Correct any failures and test again",,"A sample is restored in isolation, checked, recorded and, when it fails, corrected and tested again.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:vulnerability-management","Place the steps of the vulnerability management cycle in the correct order.",,"Discover the assets","Scan them for vulnerabilities","Prioritize the findings by risk","Remediate or mitigate","Rescan to verify",,"The cycle finds assets, scans, prioritizes, fixes and verifies with a new scan, and then repeats.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:forensics","Place the types of evidence in the correct order of volatility, from most to least volatile.",,"CPU registers and cache","Memory","Running processes and network connections","Disk contents","Remote logs","Archival media","The most volatile data disappears first and is collected first, from registers and memory to disks, remote logs and archives.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.11|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:disaster-recovery","Place the steps of a disaster recovery response in the correct order.",,"Declare the disaster","Activate the recovery team","Assess the damage","Restore the critical systems","Communicate with the stakeholders","Return to normal operations and review the response","A disaster is declared and the team is activated and assesses the damage before systems are restored in priority order, and the return to normal is reviewed.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-coding","Place the steps of handling user input securely in the correct order.",,"Receive the input","Canonicalize it","Validate it against an allow list","Use it only through parameterized queries","Encode it on output",,"Input is normalized before validation so that tricks cannot bypass checks, it is used only as data, and output is encoded for its context.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sdlc","Place the security activities of a development project in the correct order.",,"Define the security requirements","Build a threat model","Review the design","Implement the controls","Test and verify them",,"Requirements and threats are known before the design is reviewed, and the controls are built and then verified.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit-trail","Place the steps of a change that leaves a complete audit trail in the correct order.",,"A developer commits the change","A peer reviews it","Automated tests run","The approval is recorded","The change is deployed","The deployment is logged","Review, testing and approval are recorded before deployment, and the deployment itself is logged, so that every step can be audited.",2,2

slotfill[20]{tags,text,exhibit,a1,a2,a3,a4,a5,d1,d2,d3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:risk","Fill in each blank with the risk treatment that matches the description.","```text\nTo reduce risk with controls: ⟦1⟧\nTo shift the financial impact to another party: ⟦2⟧\nTo stop the activity that creates the risk: ⟦3⟧\n```","mitigate","transfer","avoid",,,"accept","ignore","reject","Mitigation adds controls, transfer shifts the impact, for example through insurance, and avoidance ends the activity. Acceptance keeps the risk, and ignoring it is not a valid treatment.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.8|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:personnel-security","Fill in each blank with the personnel security control.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ agreement protects confidential information.\n⟦2⟧ checks the background of a candidate before hiring.\nAn ⟦3⟧ interview reminds a leaving employee of continuing obligations.\n```","non-disclosure","Screening","exit",,,"service-level","Rotation","entrance","A non-disclosure agreement protects information, screening happens before hiring, and an exit interview closes the relationship. A service-level agreement, rotation and an entrance interview do not match these descriptions.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cia","Fill in each blank with the security property that the control mainly supports.","```text\nEncryption mainly supports ⟦1⟧.\nHashing mainly supports ⟦2⟧.\nRedundancy mainly supports ⟦3⟧.\n```","confidentiality","integrity","availability",,,"authenticity","non-repudiation","accountability","Encryption keeps data secret, hashing detects change, and redundancy keeps services available. The other properties are supported by different controls.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-protection","Fill in each blank with the data protection technique.","```text\n⟦1⟧ replaces a value with a random surrogate that can be mapped back.\n⟦2⟧ irreversibly removes identifying detail.\n⟦3⟧ replaces direct identifiers but allows re-identification with extra information.\n```","Tokenization","Anonymization","Pseudonymization",,,"Masking","Hashing","Encryption","Tokenization keeps a secure mapping, anonymization cannot be reversed, and pseudonymization can be reversed by someone who holds the extra information. Masking, hashing and encryption are different techniques.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:asset-management","Fill in each blank with the asset type or role.","```text\nA server is a ⟦1⟧ asset.\nA patent is an ⟦2⟧ asset.\nThe ⟦3⟧ is accountable for an asset and its protection.\n```","tangible","intangible","owner",,,"virtual","custodian","auditor","Physical items are tangible, rights and know-how are intangible, and the owner is accountable. A custodian operates the protection, and an auditor reviews it.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cryptography","Fill in each blank with the property of the algorithm.","```text\nAES encrypts blocks of ⟦1⟧ bits.\nSHA-256 produces a digest of ⟦2⟧ bits.\nElliptic curve cryptography reaches comparable strength with ⟦3⟧ keys than RSA.\n```","128","256","shorter",,,"64","160","longer","AES uses 128-bit blocks, SHA-256 produces a 256-bit digest, and elliptic curve keys are shorter than RSA keys of similar strength. The other values do not match.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:fire-suppression","Fill in each blank with the fire class.","```text\nClass ⟦1⟧ fires involve ordinary combustibles such as wood and paper.\nClass ⟦2⟧ fires involve flammable liquids.\nClass ⟦3⟧ fires involve energized electrical equipment.\n```","A","B","C",,,"D","K","E","In the North American scheme, class A is ordinary combustibles, class B is flammable liquids and class C is electrical equipment. Class D is metals and class K is cooking oils.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:tpm|topic:hsm","Fill in each blank with the hardware component.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ is a chip on the motherboard that stores keys and platform measurements.\nAn ⟦2⟧ is a dedicated appliance that performs cryptographic operations and protects keys.\n```","TPM","HSM",,,,"NIC","UPS","SIEM","A TPM is part of the computer, and a hardware security module is a separate device or card for cryptographic operations. A NIC, a UPS and a SIEM are unrelated.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ip","Fill in each blank with the address property.","```text\nIPv4 addresses are ⟦1⟧ bits long.\nIPv6 addresses are ⟦2⟧ bits long.\nAn ⟦3⟧ address is delivered to the nearest of several hosts.\n```","32","128","anycast",,,"64","multicast","broadcast","IPv4 addresses have 32 bits and IPv6 addresses have 128 bits. Anycast delivers to the nearest member of a group, while multicast delivers to all members and IPv6 has no broadcast.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:switching","Fill in each blank with the switching method.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ switch starts forwarding after reading the destination address, which gives low latency.\nA ⟦2⟧ switch receives the whole frame and checks its ⟦3⟧ before it forwards.\n```","cut-through","store-and-forward","FCS",,,"promiscuous","VLAN","TTL","Cut-through forwarding is fast but cannot check errors, while store-and-forward waits for the full frame and checks the frame check sequence. The other words do not fit.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:biometrics","Fill in each blank with the biometric term.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ occurs when an unauthorized person is accepted.\nA ⟦2⟧ occurs when an authorized person is rejected.\nThe point where the two rates are equal is the ⟦3⟧.\n```","false acceptance","false rejection","crossover error rate",,,"enrollment","throughput","registration","False acceptance and false rejection are the two error types, and the crossover error rate is where they meet. Enrollment, throughput and registration are not error types.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:provisioning","Fill in each blank with the stage of the identity lifecycle.","```text\n⟦1⟧: a new employee gets the access of the role.\n⟦2⟧: access is adjusted when the employee changes role.\n⟦3⟧: all access is removed when the employee departs.\n```","Joiner","Mover","Leaver",,,"Visitor","Contractor","Manager","The joiner, mover and leaver stages describe the lifecycle of an identity. The other words are types of people, not stages.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:oauth","Fill in each blank with the role in OAuth 2.0.","```text\nThe ⟦1⟧ issues access tokens.\nThe ⟦2⟧ hosts the protected API.\nThe ⟦3⟧ is the application that requests access on behalf of the user.\n```","authorization server","resource server","client",,,"user agent","proxy","identity provider","The authorization server issues tokens, the resource server accepts them, and the client uses them. A user agent, a proxy and an identity provider are other components.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cvss","Fill in each blank with the scoring facts of CVSS.","```text\nCVSS scores range from ⟦1⟧ to ⟦2⟧.\nA score of ⟦3⟧ or higher is rated critical.\n```","0","10","9.0",,,"5","100","7.0","CVSS scores run from 0 to 10, and a score of 9.0 or more is rated critical. 7.0 is the lower limit of the high rating.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit","Fill in each blank with the type of audit or assessment.","```text\nAn ⟦1⟧ audit is performed by the organization's own staff.\nAn ⟦2⟧ audit is performed by an independent party for stakeholders.\nA ⟦3⟧ assessment examines the controls of a supplier.\n```","internal","external","third-party",,,"continuous","compliance","synthetic","Internal audits use the organization's staff, external audits use independent auditors, and third-party assessments look at suppliers. The other words describe other kinds of checks.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:allowlist","Fill in each blank with the control.","```text\nAn ⟦1⟧ permits only the listed items to run.\nA ⟦2⟧ blocks the listed items.\nA ⟦3⟧ isolates untrusted code while it runs.\n```","allowlist","denylist","sandbox",,,"honeypot","firewall","proxy","An allowlist is a default-deny approach, a denylist is default-allow with blocks, and a sandbox contains untrusted code. A honeypot is a decoy, and a firewall and a proxy are not these controls.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:backup","Fill in each blank with the backup type.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ backup copies all selected data.\nA ⟦2⟧ backup copies the changes since the last full backup.\nAn ⟦3⟧ backup copies the changes since the last backup of any type.\n```","full","differential","incremental",,,"mirror","snapshot","archive","A full backup copies everything, a differential copies what changed since the last full backup, and an incremental copies what changed since the last backup of any type. Mirrors, snapshots and archives are different concepts.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:incident-response","Fill in each blank with the term.","```text\nAn ⟦1⟧ is any observable occurrence in a system.\nAn ⟦2⟧ is an event that violates policy or threatens security.\nA ⟦3⟧ is an incident in which data is confirmed to have been exposed.\n```","event","incident","breach",,,"alert","threat","risk","An event is any occurrence, an incident is an event with a security impact, and a breach is a confirmed exposure of data. An alert, a threat and a risk are different notions.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-coding|topic:xss|topic:csrf","Fill in each blank with the defense.","```text\nCross-site scripting is prevented by output ⟦1⟧.\nCross-site request forgery is prevented by anti-forgery ⟦2⟧.\nSQL injection is prevented by ⟦3⟧ queries.\n```","encoding","tokens","parameterized",,,"compression","cookies","hashed","Output encoding stops injected script from running, anti-forgery tokens show that a request came from the real form, and parameterized queries keep input as data. Compression and hashing do not defend against these attacks.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:api-security","Fill in each blank with the API control.","```text\n⟦1⟧ limits the number of requests per client.\n⟦2⟧ checks that the caller may perform the action.\n⟦3⟧ checks the format and range of the input.\n```","Rate limiting","Authorization","Validation",,,"Caching","Obfuscation","Tokenization","Rate limiting protects against abuse, authorization checks permissions, and validation checks the input. Caching, obfuscation and tokenization are different techniques.",2,2
